{"context":"Engineering Knowledge is immutable (ADR-011): a knowledge object is written once, content-addressed, and never mutated. Updating knowledge is a **new instance** of the same identity line — the draft-publish workflow assigns `max(existing instance versions of the line) + 1` at publish (P7 forward-only; sanctioned transitions likewise land in a newer immutable payload). Publication is therefore the **only** path by which a line's knowledge changes, and each publication makes the line's newest instance the current state of that knowledge.\n\nThe consumption contract did not follow. **This ADR amends ADR-015.** ADR-015 §Decision 3(a) established that the qualified line form resolves to the line's **lowest** instance-version:\n\n\u003e *\"the qualified line form (`\u003cns\u003e/\u003ctype\u003e:\u003cid\u003e`, the lowest instance-version of the line)\"*\n\nADR-012's rendering rule followed the same direction, appending the instance version to a same-namespace line-form target \"exactly when the target is not the line's lowest instance\". With immutable objects, \"lowest\" is the **oldest** revision — the opposite of what a consumer asking for the line means. Every consumer of the line form — `eka get`, `eka context`, `Relations.Upstream`/`Downstream`, projections, the ticket derivation, the board — saw the knowledge as it was when the line was first published, not as it is now.\n\nThe symptoms are real:\n\n1. **A work item with two instances shows stale state on the board.** The line's second instance carries the current execution state; the board resolves the line form to the first instance and displays the pre-revision state.\n2. **Domain queries return duplicates.** `eka get \u003cdomain\u003e` returns every instance of every line — the same identity line appears once per revision, and `count` counts instances, not knowledge objects.\n3. **Agents and contexts consume outdated knowledge.** AI consumers (the Context Engine of ADR-021, the skill pack of ADR-022) and relationship traversal resolve line-form targets and silently work against superseded revisions — the exact failure mode the immutable model was built to prevent, surfaced through the retrieval surface itself.","decision":"1. **The qualified line form (`\u003cns\u003e/\u003ctype\u003e:\u003cid\u003e`) resolves to the line's HIGHEST (latest) instance-version in every consumer.** The Resolver contract (`Resolver.Resolve`) picks the latest instance; graph/projection consumers (board, ticket derivation, relationship resolution) and machine/container consumers (`eka get` identity lookups, `eka context` subjects, `Relations` traversal) inherit the same semantics. Resolution is deterministic by construction: a line's instances are ordered by instance-version ascending, and the line form selects the **last**.\n2. **The versioned canonical form (`\u003cns\u003e/\u003ctype\u003e:\u003cid\u003e:\u003cv\u003e`) remains an exact-instance lookup**, unchanged. Consumers that need a specific revision address it explicitly; the meaning of the canonical form never depends on the line's other instances.\n3. **History stays reachable through explicit versions and the line's timeline.** `ResolveLine` (ascending, history order) and `Timeline.Line` are unchanged; `eka get --timeline` remains the history surface. Nothing is lost by the flip: the oldest revision remains one explicit `eka get \u003cns\u003e/\u003ctype\u003e:\u003cid\u003e:1` away.\n4. **Domain queries are deduplicated per identity line.** `eka get \u003cdomain\u003e` returns **one unit per line — the highest (latest) instance**; `count` is the number of unique lines, never the number of instances.\n5. **Reference rendering stays lossless under the new semantics.** A same-namespace reference rendered as a line form appends the explicit version **exactly when the target is not the line's highest instance** (amending ADR-012's \"not the line's lowest instance\" rule). Because the line form now resolves to the highest instance, a bare line form renders only when it resolves to exactly the referenced unit; any other target carries its version explicitly — resolution-equivalent for conformant repositories.","consequences":"- **Positive — consumers always see the newest knowledge.** Every line-form consumer (`get`, `context`, traversal, projections, board, ticket derivation) reads the latest revision of the line — the state after the last publish or transition.\n- **Positive — line-form relationships follow revisions automatically.** A relationship written against the line tracks the knowledge's evolution: when the target is republished, consumers resolving the line form see the new instance without any authoring change (line references are the norm — this is what \"latest wins\" buys).\n- **Positive — determinism is preserved.** Resolution is a pure function of the store state: instances sorted ascending, pick last. Identical synced store state → identical results, matching the Runtime determinism contract (runtime-api.md §3.3).\n- **Negative / trade-off — a line-form reference changes meaning when a new instance is published.** This is the documented, intended behavior (\"latest wins\"): the line form means \"the line as it is now\". A consumer that must pin a revision uses the versioned canonical form `\u003cns\u003e/\u003ctype\u003e:\u003cid\u003e:\u003cv\u003e` — exact instance, stable forever; scripts that want stability across revisions should address explicit versions from the start.\n- **Negative / trade-off — ADR-015's query-model text and ADR-012's rendering rule are amended.** Older readers of those ADRs must read this document to know the current contract.\n\n### References\n\n- [ADR-015](adr-015-machine-retrieval-interface.md) — the ADR this one amends: §Decision 3(a) established lowest-instance line-form resolution; the query model it defines is superseded by this ADR on that point\n- [ADR-011](adr-011-immutable-engineering-knowledge-model.md) — the immutable model that makes a new instance the only update path (P7 forward-only)\n- [ADR-012](adr-012-canonical-knowledge-object-runtime.md) — the reference-rendering rule amended by Decision 5 (lowest → highest instance)\n- [ADR-013](adr-013-store-backed-projections.md) — store-backed projections (board, ticket derivation) that consume line-form resolution\n- [ADR-021](adr-021-context-engine.md) — the Context Engine, a line-form consumer that must see the latest instance\n- The Resolver contract and the machine-retrieval consumption table: [`../runtime-api.md`](../runtime-api.md)\n- The query model, subject grammar and retrieval options: [`../cli.md`](../cli.md)\n- ADR index: [`../adr-summary.md`](../adr-summary.md)","alternativesConsidered":"- **Keep the lowest-instance rule (status quo)** — rejected: it contradicts the meaning of publication under an immutable model (P7 forward-only: instance+1 = newest knowledge) and produces the three symptoms above — stale boards, duplicate domain queries, outdated agent context.\n- **Require the versioned form everywhere (no line-form resolution)** — rejected: line references are the authoring norm and the projection/render convention; forcing exact versions onto every reference and target would break the authoring model and the relationship surface for no gain.\n- **Ambiguity refusal for multi-instance lines** — rejected: every revised line would refuse, making revision impossible to consume; the line form's job is to mean \"the line as it is now\"."}